        <h1>Dead Hangs for Shoulder Health: What the Evidence Shows</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">Overhead hanging stretches the shoulder and can be scaled to how much load you tolerate. It is not a proven treatment for impingement or rotator cuff problems.</p>
      </header>
    </div>
    <article class="article">
      <div class="article-meta">
        <a href="/about/" class="author-link">
          <span class="author-avatar">DH</span>
          <span>DeadHangs Team</span>
        </a>
        <span class="meta-sep">·</span>
        <span class="updated-date">Updated October 30, 2026</span>
        
      </div>
      <div class="container">

        <h2>What Dead Hangs Do for the Shoulder</h2>
        <p>A dead hang puts the shoulder in full flexion with the arm overhead and the joint capsule stretched under bodyweight. Hanging is thought to widen the subacromial space. This is the gap under the acromion bone where the rotator cuff tendons pass. No trial found for this page has measured that gap during a hang.</p>
        <p>The inferior shoulder capsule can tighten in people who rarely raise their arms overhead, which is common with desk work, driving and phone use. Hanging stretches the inferior capsule. No study has shown that this changes how the humeral head sits in the glenoid socket.</p>
        <p>Overhead hanging may also stretch the pectoralis minor. This chest muscle shortens when the shoulders round forward. Overhead range varies from person to person. A hang is one way to practice reaching overhead.</p>

        <h2>The Kirsch Hanging Theory</h2>
        <p>Orthopedic surgeon John M. Kirsch proposed overhead hanging for shoulder impingement in his self-published book "Shoulder Pain? The Solution &amp; Prevention" (5th ed. 2019). No published randomized trial has tested it (Europe PMC search, October 2026).</p>
        <p>Kirsch proposed that the coracoacromial arch remodels under sustained tensile load. He proposed that the acromion gradually changes shape under the pull of the deltoid and supraspinatus during a hang. He suggested this remodeling increases the subacromial space. This is his hypothesis rather than established science.</p>

        <h2>Dead Hangs for Shoulder Impingement</h2>
        <p>Shoulder impingement occurs when the supraspinatus tendon is pinched between the humeral head and the acromion. Arm elevation narrows this space. Pain shows up as a sharp catch during overhead movements and an ache at rest.</p>
        <p>Three mechanisms have been proposed for how hanging might help impingement. One is distraction of the humeral head away from the acromion. Another is stretch of the tight inferior capsule. The third is gradual remodeling of the acromion in Kirsch's hypothesis. None has been confirmed in a trial.</p>
        <p>Some people with impingement pain who try hanging start with 5 to 10 second holds with the feet on the ground to take part of the bodyweight. Ask a clinician before hanging with impingement. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription. Some dull aching can occur in the first few sessions. Sharp or worsening pain means the load is too high. Reduce the duration or the bodyweight you carry. See a physiotherapist if symptoms increase despite modifications.</p>

        <h2>Dead Hangs and the Rotator Cuff</h2>
        <p>The rotator cuff comprises four muscles: supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis. No trial has tested whether a dead hang repairs or strengthens a rotator cuff tendon.</p>
        <p>Tendinopathy means chronic tendon pain and degeneration. Isometric loading is used for tendon pain. It has not been shown to be better than other exercise. It has not been tested for dead hangs.</p>
        <p>Partial tears need a clinician's assessment before any overhead hanging. Large or full-thickness tears can worsen with overhead traction. Medical imaging determines tear size and guides the decision.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Medical Guidance Required</h3>
          <p>Ask a physician or physiotherapist before using dead hangs with a rotator cuff injury. Imaging results determine whether hanging is appropriate for your tear type and size. Hanging in place of proper treatment can delay care for a significant tear.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Many people start with <a href="/passive-vs-active-hang/">passive hangs</a>. These load the arm mostly through passive tissues. Active hangs with the shoulder blades pulled down ask the shoulder muscles to work. Some people add them once passive hangs are pain free.</p>

        <h2>Shoulder Mobility Routine</h2>
        <p>A hang pairs well with mobility drills that target the muscle groups limiting overhead range. This four-exercise routine is common training practice. It is not a tested prescription.</p>

        <h3>Dead Hang (Primary)</h3>
        <p>Perform 3 to 4 sets of 15 to 30 seconds. Relax the shoulders and breathe. A passive hang stretches the capsule, lats and pectoralis minor. Rest 60 seconds between sets.</p>

        <h3>Wall Slides</h3>
        <p>Stand with your back flat against a wall. Place your arms in a "goalpost" position with elbows at 90 degrees. Slide your arms up the wall until they straighten overhead. Return to the start position. Perform 10 to 15 repetitions. Wall slides train scapular upward rotation and serratus anterior activation.</p>

        <h3>Band Pull-Aparts</h3>
        <p>Hold a resistance band at chest height with straight arms. Pull the band apart by squeezing your shoulder blades together. Return slowly. Perform 15 to 20 repetitions. Band pull-aparts work the rhomboids and posterior deltoids. These muscles counterbalance tight anterior muscles.</p>

        <h3>External Rotation</h3>
        <p>Hold a light resistance band with your elbow pinned to your side at 90 degrees. Rotate your forearm outward against the band's resistance. Control the return. Perform 15 repetitions per side. External rotation targets the infraspinatus and teres minor.</p>

        <h2>Active vs Passive Hang for Shoulders</h2>
        <p>A passive dead hang lets the shoulder muscles relax. Bodyweight then stretches the joint capsule, ligaments and surrounding muscles. A passive hang suits mobility work. An active dead hang pulls the shoulder blades down and back without bending the elbows, which engages the lower trapezius, serratus anterior and rotator cuff stabilizers.</p>
        <p>A common approach is to start with passive hangs until overhead range feels comfortable and pain free. Add active hangs once you can hold about 30 seconds passively without discomfort. The passive vs active hang guide in the Related Guides below compares the two in full.</p>

        <h2>When to Avoid Hanging</h2>
        <p>Hanging is not suitable for every shoulder. Avoid it in these situations. Ask a clinician before you start.</p>

        <h3>Acute Tears</h3>
        <p>A fresh rotator cuff or labral tear needs rest and medical assessment. Hanging places traction force directly through the injured tissue. Wait for your physician to clear overhead loading before attempting any dead hang variation.</p>

        <h3>Post-Surgical</h3>
        <p>Shoulder surgery repairs or reconstructs damaged structures. Follow your surgeon's rehabilitation timeline. Hanging too early risks re-tearing the repair.</p>

        <h3>Unstable Shoulder</h3>
        <p>Recurrent shoulder dislocations indicate ligament laxity. Hanging stretches the capsule and ligaments further. This can increase instability rather than reduce it. Strengthening exercises under physiotherapy guidance take priority over stretching for unstable shoulders.</p>

        <h3>Severe Pain During a Hang</h3>
        <p>Sharp, catching or electrical pain during a dead hang is a signal to stop. Release the bar and reduce the load by keeping your feet on the ground. Get a clinical assessment if the pain persists at any load.</p>

        <h2>Shoulder-Safe Dead Hang Progression</h2>
        <p>This four-stage shoulder progression runs from a wall hang to a full dead hang. The durations and frequencies are common training practice. They are not a tested prescription. Move to the next stage only when you complete the current one pain free.</p>

        <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table>
          <thead>
