        <p>A band-assisted hang uses a resistance band looped over the bar with your foot or knee in the band. Thicker bands take more of your weight. This reduces the traction force through the shoulder. Check the band's rated assistance and record the assist you used so later hangs stay comparable.</p>
        <p>People with existing shoulder issues may need longer on each stage. A physiotherapist can set the pace.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Are dead hangs good for shoulder impingement?</h3>
          <p>Orthopedic surgeon John M. Kirsch proposed overhead hanging for shoulder impingement in a self-published book (5th ed. 2019). No published randomized trial has tested it (Europe PMC search, October 2026). If you try short holds then stop when you feel sharp pain and see a clinician if it persists.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Should I do passive or active hangs for shoulder health?</h3>
          <p>A passive hang stretches the shoulder with the muscles relaxed. An active hang asks the shoulder blade muscles to work. Many people start passive and add active hangs once a 30 second passive hold feels comfortable. A physiotherapist can set the pace if you are recovering from an injury.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Can dead hangs help a rotator cuff injury?</h3>
          <p>Dead hangs have not been tested as a treatment for rotator cuff injury. Partial tears need a clinician's assessment before any overhead hanging. Full-thickness tears require medical evaluation first. Ask a physician or physiotherapist before hanging with a diagnosed rotator cuff injury.</p>
        </div>

